course: dictionary
lesson: 13628
title: One at a time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Individually is an adverb. It tells you how something is done: one at a time, on its own, not as a group. «The teacher checked each pupil's work individually.» Each pupil's work was checked alone, one piece after another.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «The sweets were sold individually.» Sweets sold in a pack would come as a group, so sold individually means each sweet was sold on its own. Try swapping in «one by one»: if it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: The second meaning is about fitting each single thing. «Each cake was individually decorated.» Every cake got its own design, made for that one cake, not copied from the others.
fact: Individually is an adverb, so it describes how an action happens.
fact: «Individually» means one at a time, separately, not as a group.
fact: «Individually» can also mean done in a way that fits each single person or thing.
fact: A good check is to swap in «one by one» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: The root word is individual, which means a single person or thing.
exercise: «The teacher checked each pupil's work individually.» What does individually mean here? | In a big pack with many others | *One at a time, on its own | Together as a whole class | Very quickly and loudly
exercise: «Each cake was individually decorated.» What does individually suggest? | The cakes were thrown away | The cakes were eaten in one go | All the cakes had the same plain icing | *Each cake got its own special design
exercise: Which word can you swap in for individually in «The sweets were sold individually»? | never | very loudly | *one by one | all together
test: «Please read the story individually, then tell a partner what you think.» What does individually mean? | *Alone, each person on their own | Quietly, with the lights off | As one big group | Very fast
test: Ella says individually means «in a big pack». Is she right? | *No, it means one at a time or separately | Yes, it means very loud | Yes, it means all together | No, it means very happy
exam: «The biscuits were individually wrapped, so Sam could eat one and keep the rest fresh.» What does individually show here? | The biscuits were given away | The biscuits were burned | All the biscuits were wrapped in one big bag | *Each biscuit was wrapped on its own
